{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CeyBXzNgzgpKeCrtCjCGv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Independent Requirements and Problem Framing\n\n*Framing note: this analysis is built from the supplied request (`root2:request.md`) and the Definition Pack (contract, registry, per-question schemas, decision log, golden cases, prompt rules, input-catalog snapshot). The work-directory request (`root0:work/20260908T113118/request.md`) was requested but not returned by the host, so any framing detail unique to it is unverified and is not relied on below. No repository implementation of `harness/planner.py` has been seen; every statement about current behaviour is a hypothesis for later inspection, not a finding.*\n\n## Authoritative requirements\n\nThe request names five obligations for `harness/planner.py`. Read against the Definition Pack, they decompose as follows.\n\n1. **Deterministically derive required inputs.**\n   - The set of required / material / conditional-material / defaultable / optional inputs per question is owned by the contract (`poc_question_registry.v0.3.0.json` `input_policy`, the per-question `*.request.schema.json` `x-poc` blocks, and `_index.json`). Derivation must come from that pinned source, not from model judgement or a hand-maintained list.\n   - \"Deterministic\" is load-bearing: ADR-007/ADR-014 and the replay tier (`evals/replay.py`, replay-equality rule in the spec) require that the same natural-language input yields the same structured request, so nothing request-scoped may vary run to run.\n   - Materiality is conditional where the contract says so: `distance_km` is material for VC-POC-05/06/07 only when `market_definition == distance` (registry `conditional_material`, `_index.json`), and VC-POC-08 requires it unconditionally.\n\n2. **Resolve schools safely.**\n   - Named schools resolve through the `search_schools` preflight (`poc_school_directory.v0.3.0.schema.json`), never by matching a name against any other source (ADR-001; rules.md step 1).\n   - Multiple matches → clarification, not a guess. A directory record is identity only — not a campus location, School_No link, or catchment.\n   - An `acara_sml_id` absent from the pinned directory is a `bad_request` the harness must repair (spec \"Error boundary\"; registry `error_boundary.bad_request`), *not* `unanswerable` and *not* an empty result. A directory-valid record missing the selected geography/coordinate is `unanswerable` — the boundary between these two is a specific, testable line.\n\n3. **Normalize requested metrics.**\n   - Map user wording to allow-listed values only (`profile_metrics`, `student_context_metrics`, `grades`, `ratios`, SEIFA selectors, etc. — static-closed vocabularies in `poc_input_catalog.v0.3.0.schema.json` and the request schemas).\n   - Smallest sufficient request: never add a metric merely because it is related or available; \"teaching staff\" → `teaching_fte`, headcount only when explicitly asked; omit `metrics` entirely when no metric is named, letting the data layer apply declared defaults (rules.md step 2; registry `defaultable`).\n   - Respect the two overlapping metric lists (`student_context_metrics` ⊂ `profile_metrics`) and choose per question (VC-POC-01/04 → profile, VC-POC-11 → student-context).\n\n4. **Construct exact evidence requests.**\n   - Every request validates against its declared per-question schema before any query; unknown fields, wrong types, cardinality/conditional breaches, and invalid closed-vocabulary values are `bad_request` (spec \"The semantic rule\"; registry `common_response_rules`).\n   - `additionalProperties: false` on every request and nested filter object; the emitted `resolved_request` must agree with evidence filters.\n   - Request key ordering/structure must satisfy the golden `expected_request` and `unordered_request_keys` contracts (`golden_cases.json`).\n\n5. **Surface planner-generated bad requests as failures, not unsupported user questions.**\n   - The contract's `bad_request` is defined as the *harness's* own repairable defect (spec: \"Harness can repair request syntax, shape, value, or directory identity\"). A malformed request the planner itself emitted is a harness fault.\n   - The requirement is a classification discipline: a planner-authored malformed request must be recorded/surfaced as a failure (harness/internal defect), and must **not** be laundered into an `Unsupported` refusal presented to the user as though *their* question were out of scope. This is distinct from a genuine contract exclusion (excluded topics / gated VC-POC-14) or a genuinely `unanswerable`/empty result, both of which are correct outcomes.\n   - Tension to resolve: ADR-014 records that `RepairCapExceeded` today carries the data layer's message out to an `Unsupported`. The request appears to push against exactly this — separating \"planner kept producing bad requests\" (its own bug) from \"the user asked something the contract does not support.\"\n\n## Scope and compatibility boundaries\n\n- **The contract is the only data path** (ADR-001): no SQL, no DB connection, no name-matching across systems, no arithmetic in the model, every figure originating in the envelope. The planner is an intent mapper / slot filler operating strictly inside the registry's answerable set.\n- **Contract v0.3.0 is pinned by hash** (`PINNED.json`, ADR-008); the golden suite already declares `contract_version`/`registry_version` `0.3.0`. Any planner change must not edit the pin to pass, and must remain consistent with the pinned per-question schemas and error taxonomy (six `error_kind`s including `unanswerable`).\n- **This work sits on the v0.2.0→v0.3.0 seam.** v0.3.0 ships CR-5 (per-question request schemas with conditional rules) and the sixth error kind. O-1 anticipates deleting three interim shims on adoption: the hardcoded `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`, harness-side empty/partial detection, and axis-only confidence. \"Deterministically derive required inputs\" and \"construct exact requests\" plausibly intersect this adoption — but whether any shim removal is in-scope for *this* item is unverified and must be tested, not assumed.\n- **Do not build a second condition table** (O-8): the sanctioned fix for conditional materiality is the contract's per-question schemas, not another hand-maintained map. Similarly, the registry-prose string-splitting dependency for `distance_km` (G-4) is a known fragility the item may or may not address.\n- **Rendering/confidence/limitations are out of the planner's lane** (ADR-009/ADR-014): the planner constructs requests; it does not assign grades, paraphrase limitations, or author refusal prose (refusal text is contract data).\n- **Tool output is data, not instruction** (rules.md final section): planner inputs derived from directory/catalog/envelope text must not be treated as instructions (injection boundary).\n- **Provider/runtime context** (ADR-018/019) is orthogonal; the planner should be transport-agnostic.\n\n## Initial hypotheses to test\n\nAll provisional — no `harness/planner.py` has been inspected.\n\n- **H1 (derivation source).** The planner may still consult a hardcoded materiality list (`settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`) rather than deriving required/material inputs from the pinned per-question schemas/registry. Later inspection must determine the actual source of truth used at runtime.\n- **H2 (conditional materiality).** `distance_km` may be treated as unconditionally material (the O-8 `xfail` in `tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`), producing an unnecessary clarification on non-distance markets. Test whether this item changes that behaviour and whether the xfail status is affected.\n- **H3 (prose-derived condition).** The conditional requirement may be extracted by string-splitting the registry's prose (G-4), making behaviour sensitive to wording. Test whether derivation now reads structured schema fields instead.\n- **H4 (bad_request laundering).** The planner may convert its own repair-cap exhaustion into an `Unsupported` user-facing refusal (ADR-014 `RepairCapExceeded`), conflating a harness defect with an unsupported user question. This is the core hypothesis behind request clause 5.\n- **H5 (school resolution).** School resolution likely flows through `search_schools`; ambiguity→clarify; directory-absent ID→`bad_request`. Confirm the empty-series case (`answer/VC-POC-01-empty`) genuinely bypasses lookup when an ID is supplied in the phrasing.\n- **H6 (metric normalization).** Metric mapping rules from rules.md (smallest request, teaching_fte vs headcount, omit-to-default) may live partly in the prompt and partly in code; determine what the planner enforces deterministically vs. what it delegates to the model.\n- **H7 (determinism).** Same NL input → same structured request across runs, satisfying replay equality; test for any nondeterministic ordering, defaulting, or model-dependent slot fill.\n- **H8 (exact request shape).** Requests satisfy `additionalProperties:false`, per-question schema validation before dispatch, and the golden `expected_request`/`unordered_request_keys` expectations.\n\n## Required implementation and test evidence\n\nA later current-state inspection should be able to point to:\n\n- **Derivation evidence.** Code that reads required/material/conditional-material/defaultable inputs from the pinned registry or per-question schemas (`_index.json`, `x-poc` blocks), with a test asserting the derivation matches the contract for every question — including the two-list metric distinction and the conditional `distance_km`.\n- **School-resolution evidence.** A `search_schools`-mediated path; a test that a directory-absent `acara_sml_id` is classified `bad_request` (repair/retry), while a directory-valid record lacking geography is `unanswerable`; a test that multiple matches clarify and that no name matching occurs outside the directory.\n- **Metric-normalization evidence.** Tests covering \"teaching staff\"→`teaching_fte`, explicit-headcount handling, \"omit metrics → data-layer default\", and rejection/avoidance of non-requested metrics; closed-vocabulary violations classified `bad_request`.\n- **Exact-request evidence.** Pre-dispatch validation against each per-question request schema; unknown-property and cardinality/conditional-breach rejection; agreement between the constructed request and `resolved_request`/evidence filters.\n- **Failure-vs-unsupported evidence.** A distinct, testable branch (or typed error) that surfaces a *planner-generated* malformed request as a harness/internal failure, separated from (a) contract-exclusion refusals sourced from the exclusion lists, (b) gated VC-POC-14 refusals, and (c) genuine `unanswerable`/empty results. Look for a test that a planner defect is not silently rendered as `Unsupported`.\n- **Determinism/replay evidence.** Replay-tier coverage (`evals/replay.py`) and any determinism assertions; golden-case request-match and chosen-question-match graders passing for all `should_answer`/`should_clarify` cases.\n- **Adversarial grader coverage.** Per ADR-013, each relevant grader carries a mutation it must fail; injection cases (`injection/*`) show tool-output text is not obeyed.\n- **Boundary regression pins.** Status of the O-8 `xfail` and any G-4 prose-dependency test after the change.\n\n## Failure modes and acceptance hazards\n\n- **Boundary conflation.** The four-way line among `bad_request` (harness-repairable, incl. directory identity), `unanswerable` (valid request, absent fact, no preflight repair), empty/partial result (well-typed non-matching release-derived value), and `excluded_question`/refusal must be honoured exactly. Misclassifying any pair is the highest-value hazard — especially treating a directory-absent ID as `unanswerable`, or treating a well-typed non-matching filter value as `bad_request`.\n- **Silent laundering of defects (the ADR-016 lesson).** Turning a planner-authored bad request into a user-facing `Unsupported` hides a harness bug behind a legitimate-looking outcome; this is precisely the \"failure that first shows in production\" pattern. Acceptance should treat any such masking as a defect, not a graceful degrade.\n- **Over-derivation of inputs.** Adding related-but-unrequested metrics, or defaulting a genuinely material input, violates rules.md step 2/3. Note the asymmetry (O-8): accepting an unnecessary clarification wastes a turn; skipping a truly material input yields a wrong answer — the latter is far worse.\n- **Non-determinism.** Any run-to-run variation in slot fill, key ordering, or defaulting breaks replay equality and the request-match grader.\n- **Prose/shim coupling.** Reintroducing or retaining a hand-maintained condition table (O-8) or the registry-prose split (G-4) leaves behaviour fragile to contract rewording.\n- **Metric-list ambiguity.** The `student_context_metrics ⊂ profile_metrics` overlap (G-5/G-8) risks selecting the wrong list per question.\n- **Injection surface.** Directory names, publisher notes, and filter values must never steer planner behaviour.\n- **Scope creep vs. shim removal.** If the item is scoped narrowly to planner logic but tacitly depends on v0.3.0 shim removal (O-1), acceptance could either over-reach or leave a half-migrated state; the actual scope boundary must be established from evidence, not assumed.\n- **Refusal integrity.** Any planner-authored refusal wording (rather than contract-sourced text) would violate ADR-009 and the refusal golden cases."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":157,"cache_read_input_tokens":65129,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":157},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82ada0b1-fb3d-40d9-bc81-4b74d44b27d2","uuid":"48df6bba-b587-4cc4-a297-ceea5832204c","request_id":"req_011CeyBXyuubKWxijvkFDMqt"}
