red schema fields instead.\n- **H4 (bad_request laundering).** The planner may convert its own repair-cap exhaustion into an `Unsupported` user-facing refusal (ADR-014 `RepairCapExceeded`), conflating a harness defect with an unsupported user question. This is the core hypothesis behind request clause 5.\n- **H5 (school resolution).** School resolution likely flows through `search_schools`; ambiguity→clarify; directory-absent ID→`bad_request`. Confirm the empty-series case (`answer/VC-POC-01-empty`) genuinely bypasses lookup when an ID is supplied in the phrasing.\n- **H6 (metric normalization).** Metric mapping rules from rules.md (smallest request, teaching_fte vs headcount, omit-to-default) may live partly in the prompt and partly in code; determine what the planner enforces deterministically vs. what it delegates to the model.\n- **H7 (determinism).** Same NL input → same structured request across runs, satisfying replay equality; test for any nondeterministic ordering, defaulting, or model-dependent slot fill.\n- **H8 (exact request shape).** Requests satisfy `additionalProperties:false`, per-question schema validation before dispatch, and the golden `expected_request`/`unordered_request_keys` expectations.\n\n## Required implementation and test evidence\n\nA later current-state inspection should be able to point to:\n\n- **Derivation evidence.** Code that reads required/material/conditional-material/defaultable inputs from the pinned registry or per-question schemas (`_index.json`, `x-poc` blocks), with a test asserting the derivation matches the contract for every question — including the two-list metric distinction and the conditional `distance_km`.\n- **School-resolution evidence.** A `search_schools`-mediated path; a test that a directory-absent `acara_sml_id` is classified `bad_request` (repair/retry), while a directory-valid record lacking geography is `unanswerable`; a test that multiple matches clarify and that no name matching occurs outside the directory.\n- **Metric-normalization evidence.** Tests covering \"teaching staff\"→`teaching_fte`, explicit-headcount handling, \"omit metrics → data-layer default\", and rejection/avoidance of non-requested metrics; closed-vocabulary violations classified `bad_request`.\n- **Exact-request evidence.** Pre-dispatch validation against each per-question request schema; unknown-property and cardinality/conditional-breach rejection; agreement between the constructed request and `resolved_request`/evidence filters.\n- **Failure-vs-unsupported evidence.** A distinct, testable branch (or typed error) that surfaces a *planner-generated* malformed request as a harness/internal failure, separated from (a) contract-exclusion refusals sourced from the exclusion lists, (b) gated VC-POC-14 refusals, and (c) genuine `unanswerable`/empty results. Look for a test that a planner defect is not silently rendered as `Unsupported`.\n- **Determinism/replay evidence.** Replay-tier coverage (`evals/replay.py`) and any determinism assertions; golden-case request-match and chosen-question-match graders passing for all `should_answer`/`should_clarify` cases.\n- **Adversarial grader coverage.** Per ADR-013, each relevant grader carries a mutation it must fail; injection cases (`injection/*`) show tool-output text is not obeyed.\n- **Boundary regression pins.** Status of the O-8 `xfail` and any G-4 prose-dependency test after the change.\n\n## Failure modes and acceptance hazards\n\n- **Boundary conflation.** The four-way line among `bad_request` (harness-repairable, incl. directory identity), `unanswerable` (valid request, absent fact, no preflight repair), empty/partial result (well-typed non-matching release-derived value), and `excluded_question`/refusal must be honoured exactly. Misclassifying any pair is the highest-value hazard — especially treating a directory-absent ID as `unanswerable`, or treating a well-typed non-matching filter value as `bad_request`.\n- **Silent laundering of defects (the ADR-016 lesson).** Turning a planner-authored bad request into a user-facing `Unsupported` hides a harness bug behind a legitimate-looking outcome; this is precisely the \"failure that first shows in production\" pattern. Acceptance should treat any such masking as a defect, not a graceful degrade.\n- **Over-derivation of inputs.** Adding related-but-unrequested metrics, or defaulting a genuinely material input, violates rules.md step 2/3. Note the asymmetry (O-8): accepting an unnecessary clarification wastes a turn; skipping a truly material input yields a wrong answer — the latter is far worse.\n- **Non-determinism.** Any run-to-run variation in slot fill, key ordering, or defaulting breaks replay equality and the request-match grader.\n- **Prose/shim coupling.** Reintroducing or retaining a hand-maintained condition table (O-8) or the registry-prose split (G-4) leaves behaviour fragile to contract rewording.\n- **Metric-list ambiguity.** The `student_context_metrics ⊂ profile_metrics` overlap (G-5/G-8) risks selecting the wrong list per question.\n- **Injection surface.** Directory names, publisher notes, and filter values must never steer planner behaviour.\n- **Scope creep vs. shim removal.** If the item is scoped narrowly to planner logic but tacitly depends on v0.3.0 shim removal (O-1), acceptance could either over-reach or leave a half-migrated state; the actual scope boundary must be established from evidence, not assumed.\n- **Refusal integrity.** Any planner-authored refusal wording (rather than contract-sourced text) would violate ADR-009 and the refusal golden cases.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"82ada0b1-fb3d-40d9-bc81-4b74d44b27d2","total_cost_usd":0.7190129999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65286,"cache_read_input_tokens":129501,"output_tokens":7812,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65286,"ephemeral_5m_i